<!--
     Power model for malbec.

     Two independent sources, and the split matters:

     * The SCALARS (screen, wifi, bluetooth, audio, video, camera, gps, battery)
       are decoded from the stock ZUI framework-res.apk with `aapt2 dump
       xmltree`. Those are chassis properties: this panel, this Wi-Fi front end,
       this 10200 mAh cell. Lenovo measured them on this machine.

       ⚠️ That sentence was true of every scalar EXCEPT the five
       wifi.controller.* keys, which stock sets and this file did not carry until
       session 25 — the one group Android 16 actually reads on this chip, so
       Wi-Fi drain was attributed as exactly zero. Re-derived by diffing the
       whole scalar group against stock's apk key by key; everything else matched
       byte for byte. **If you add a value here, diff the group, do not spot-check
       it.**

     * The CPU TABLES are NOT. The stock profile's cpu.speeds.cluster* lists
       (18 / 32 / 29 / 35 entries, topping out at 2265.6 / 3148.8 / 2956.8 /
       3302.4 MHz) match no cpufreq policy on this silicon. The device really
       has, read out of /sys/.../cpufreq/policyN/stats/time_in_state on the
       running unit (the authority, since scaling_available_frequencies is
       filtered at runtime by thermal and read short):

           policy0  cpu0-1   15 steps   364.8 MHz .. 2016.0 MHz
           policy2  cpu2-4   23 steps   480.0 MHz .. 3014.4 MHz
           policy5  cpu5-6   21 steps   480.0 MHz .. 2803.2 MHz
           policy7  cpu7     23 steps   480.0 MHz .. 3206.4 MHz

       Lenovo shipped another project's table on a shared system partition. The
       earlier note here checked cpu.clusters.cores (2/3/2/1) against
       /proc/cpuinfo and concluded the profile belonged to this device. The core
       counts do line up; the frequencies never did. Necessary is not sufficient.

       The frequency lists below are this unit's, verbatim. The per-step
       currents are onyx's (Redmi Turbo 4 Pro / POCO F7, the official PixelOS
       device on the SAME silicon and the SAME 2/3/2/1 cluster layout), matched
       step for step by frequency. Clusters 0, 1 and 2 line up exactly, so those
       are transplants, not estimates. Cluster 3 needed two: onyx lists 2129.6
       where this unit has 2169.6 (40 MHz apart, same rung), and onyx has no
       3168.0 step, so that one point is linearly interpolated between 3014.4
       and 3206.4. Both are marked below.

     ⚠️ The KEY NAMES changed and that was the other half of the bug. This file
     used cpu.speeds.clusterN / cpu.active.clusterN. PowerProfile.java:497-498
     reads cpu.core_speeds.clusterN / cpu.core_power.clusterN and nothing in
     frameworks/base aliases the old names, so initCpuClusters() found no data
     and initCpuScalingPolicies() fell through to stepPower = {0.0} for all four
     clusters. Roughly half this file was inert. AOSP's own sample profile still
     uses the old spelling, which is where it came from.

     Cellular is deliberately absent (radio.on / radio.active / radio.scanning /
     modem.controller.*): this is a Wi-Fi-only tablet with androidboot.vendor
     .qspa.modem=disabled and no modem blobs. A radio power model here would
     only let BatteryStats attribute drain to a radio that is not there.
-->
<device name="Android">
    <item name="ambient.on.display0">0.1</item>
    <item name="screen.on.display0">122.8</item>
    <item name="screen.full.display0">1725.8</item>
    <item name="bluetooth.active">7.41</item>
    <item name="bluetooth.on">0.56</item>
    <item name="wifi.on">2</item>
    <item name="wifi.active">77.58</item>
    <item name="wifi.scan">150.15</item>

    <!-- ★ The controller group, and on THIS device it is the only Wi-Fi group
         that gets read. Added session 25; without it, Settings > Battery
         attributed exactly nothing to Wi-Fi on a Wi-Fi-only tablet — measured,
         `dumpsys batterystats --checkin | grep '^9,0,l,pwi,wifi'` returned
         `9,0,l,pwi,wifi,0,0,0,0`.

         Android 16 has no legacy WifiPowerCalculator left (there is no
         *PowerCalculator.java in frameworks/base at all). The only path is
         WifiPowerStatsProcessor, which branches on
         `mHasWifiPowerController = mStatsLayout.isPowerReportingSupported()`,
         fed from WifiPowerStatsCollector via
         `WifiManager.isEnhancedPowerReportingSupported()` =
         `isFeatureSupported(WIFI_FEATURE_LINK_LAYER_STATS)`. This chip HAS that
         feature — `dumpsys wifi` lists WIFI_FEATURE_LINK_LAYER_STATS and reports
         real radioOnTime/radioTxTime/radioRxTime — so the controller branch is
         taken and `wifi.active` above becomes DEAD. It is kept only because it is
         stock's value and the `else` branch would need it on a chip without LLS.

         PowerProfile.getAveragePower() returns 0.0 for an absent key, and
         WifiPowerStatsProcessor's ODPM rescue cannot help either
         (`if (totalPower == 0) return 1;`), so a missing key is a silent zero
         rather than a fallback. All five values are stock's own, read out of the
         ZUI framework-res.apk with aapt2, like every other scalar in this file.

         ⚠️ wifi.controller.voltage is not decoration: BatteryStatsImpl divides
         monitoredRailChargeConsumedMaMs by it OUTSIDE the `if (opVolt != 0)`
         guard, so a missing value can put Long.MAX_VALUE into battery history the
         moment rail stats are populated. -->
    <item name="wifi.controller.idle">1</item>
    <item name="wifi.controller.rx">176</item>
    <item name="wifi.controller.tx">200</item>
    <array name="wifi.controller.tx_levels">
        <value>1</value>
    </array>
    <item name="wifi.controller.voltage">3700</item>

    <item name="audio">48</item>
